# App-level Kconfig for dualcore_remote. Presence of this file makes CMake's
# kconfig.cmake use IT as KCONFIG_ROOT instead of $ZEPHYR_BASE/Kconfig (see
# cmake/modules/kconfig.cmake in the Zephyr tree), so the trailing
# `source "Kconfig.zephyr"` below is mandatory -- without it this app would
# lose every upstream Kconfig option (LOG, IPC_SERVICE, MBOX, ...) that
# prj.conf already turns on. This mirrors ../dualcore_host/Kconfig's own
# comment for the identical reason.

config DEMO_REMOTE_BEACON
	bool "Write a liveness beacon to global SRAM0 (bench diagnostic)"
	default n
	help
	  DIAGNOSTIC ONLY -- OFF by default, not part of the shipped image.
	  Enable explicitly with `-DEXTRA_CONF_FILE=beacon.conf` (../beacon.conf),
	  the same opt-in shape dualcore_host's breadcrumb.conf uses; do not flip
	  this default instead.

	  WHY THIS EXISTS: the host's own console (see ../dualcore_host/src/main.c)
	  can prove it completed the SE core-release call, but has no way to prove
	  whether the RELEASED core (this app) ever actually ran -- the two
	  M55 clusters' local memories are not cross-readable from a debug-AP
	  session attached to the other core. A "no PONG, endpoint never binds"
	  symptom is consistent with at least three different failures (the
	  remote never executed at all; it ran but hung before opening ipc0; it
	  opened ipc0 fine but the RPMsg bind itself is broken, e.g. by cache
	  incoherency on the shared vring memory) that all look identical from
	  the host's side alone. This beacon is the observable that tells them
	  apart, by writing to global SRAM0 -- memory reachable from EITHER
	  core's debug-AP, unlike this core's own DTCM.

	  When enabled, src/main.c writes six fixed 32-bit words to global SRAM0
	  at 0x02000100-0x02000114 -- see the top-of-file comment above
	  beacon_write() in src/main.c for exactly which word means what and why
	  each address was picked. This range is deliberately DISJOINT from
	  dualcore_host's own execution-breadcrumb block (0x02000000-0x02000038,
	  see ../dualcore_host/Kconfig's CONFIG_DEMO_EXECUTION_BREADCRUMB) so a
	  bench read of global SRAM0 can never confuse the two apps' diagnostics,
	  even when both are enabled on the same silicon at once (host on one
	  cluster, remote on the other, sharing the same physical SRAM0).

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
